1
00:00:00,511 --> 00:00:02,494
Your cat lives in the same house

2
00:00:02,344 --> 00:00:05,383
as you, but it experiences a completely

3
00:00:05,331 --> 00:00:06,258
different world.

4
00:00:06,553 --> 00:00:09,647
It sees, hears and smells things you

5
00:00:09,511 --> 00:00:10,828
can't even imagine.

6
00:00:11,764 --> 00:00:15,109
Some of its senses are real superpowers.

7
00:00:15,403 --> 00:00:17,095
Others are surprisingly weak.

8
00:00:17,389 --> 00:00:20,095
And understanding them explains a lot of

9
00:00:19,959 --> 00:00:21,706
your cat's strange behavior.

10
00:00:22,644 --> 00:00:24,377
So today, let's step into your cat's

11
00:00:24,227 --> 00:00:26,655
head and find out how cats really

12
00:00:26,505 --> 00:00:30,294
see, hear, smell and feel the world.

13
00:00:31,244 --> 00:00:32,019
Chapter one:

14
00:00:32,147 --> 00:00:32,783
vision.

15
00:00:33,091 --> 00:00:35,186
Let's start with the eyes, because they're

16
00:00:35,036 --> 00:00:37,505
probably the most impressive thing about your

17
00:00:37,355 --> 00:00:37,964
cat.

18
00:00:38,911 --> 00:00:40,644
Cats can see in light about six

19
00:00:40,494 --> 00:00:42,172
times dimmer than what we need.

20
00:00:42,481 --> 00:00:44,089
That's why your cat moves through a

21
00:00:43,939 --> 00:00:46,742
dark house without bumping into anything.

22
00:00:47,711 --> 00:00:50,125
Part of the secret is a mirror-like

23
00:00:49,989 --> 00:00:51,792
layer at the back of the eye,

24
00:00:51,947 --> 00:00:53,583
called the tapetum lucidum.

25
00:00:53,864 --> 00:00:56,028
It bounces light back through the eye

26
00:00:55,878 --> 00:00:56,944
a second time.

27
00:00:57,911 --> 00:01:00,408
That layer is also why cat eyes

28
00:01:00,272 --> 00:01:01,908
glow in the dark when a light

29
00:01:01,772 --> 00:01:02,450
hits them.

30
00:01:02,744 --> 00:01:05,186
It's not magic, it's a built-in light

31
00:01:05,036 --> 00:01:05,811
booster.

32
00:01:06,792 --> 00:01:08,775
But that night vision has a price:

33
00:01:08,653 --> 00:01:09,456
colors.

34
00:01:09,750 --> 00:01:12,345
Cats see blues and greens well, but

35
00:01:12,195 --> 00:01:14,761
reds probably look dull and grayish to

36
00:01:14,611 --> 00:01:15,011
them.

37
00:01:15,978 --> 00:01:17,628
So that bright red toy you bought?

38
00:01:17,923 --> 00:01:19,559
To your cat, it might look a

39
00:01:19,409 --> 00:01:21,336
lot more boring than it does to

40
00:01:21,186 --> 00:01:21,600
you.

41
00:01:22,578 --> 00:01:25,645
Cats also don't see details as sharply

42
00:01:25,509 --> 00:01:26,284
as we do.

43
00:01:26,592 --> 00:01:29,311
Something you see clearly from far away

44
00:01:29,356 --> 00:01:31,923
looks blurry to a cat, until it

45
00:01:31,773 --> 00:01:33,186
gets much closer.

46
00:01:34,116 --> 00:01:36,683
And up close, it's even stranger.

47
00:01:36,977 --> 00:01:39,377
Cats have trouble focusing on things right

48
00:01:39,227 --> 00:01:40,239
under their nose.

49
00:01:40,533 --> 00:01:43,350
That's one reason they sniff and touch

50
00:01:43,269 --> 00:01:44,308
before they look.

51
00:01:45,278 --> 00:01:47,123
Where cats really win is motion.

52
00:01:47,403 --> 00:01:49,359
Their eyes are built to catch the

53
00:01:49,223 --> 00:01:53,192
tiniest movement, like a mouse tail twitching

54
00:01:53,042 --> 00:01:54,039
in the grass.

55
00:01:55,025 --> 00:01:56,689
That's why a toy that sits still

56
00:01:56,553 --> 00:01:59,578
is boring, and the exact same toy

57
00:01:59,511 --> 00:02:01,994
is irresistible the moment it moves.

58
00:02:02,958 --> 00:02:05,094
And cats have a wider field of

59
00:02:04,958 --> 00:02:07,400
view than we do, about two hundred

60
00:02:07,250 --> 00:02:10,247
degrees, compared with roughly one hundred eighty

61
00:02:10,097 --> 00:02:10,927
for us.

62
00:02:11,222 --> 00:02:13,414
Sneaking up on a cat is harder

63
00:02:13,264 --> 00:02:14,150
than you think.

64
00:02:15,111 --> 00:02:15,956
Chapter two:

65
00:02:16,403 --> 00:02:16,956
hearing.

66
00:02:17,264 --> 00:02:19,650
If the eyes are impressive, the ears

67
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:20,608
are on another level.

68
00:02:21,606 --> 00:02:23,645
Humans hear sounds up to about twenty

69
00:02:23,509 --> 00:02:24,645
thousand hertz.

70
00:02:24,981 --> 00:02:27,228
Cats can hear up to around sixty-four

71
00:02:27,078 --> 00:02:29,534
thousand hertz, far into what we call

72
00:02:29,481 --> 00:02:30,506
ultrasound.

73
00:02:31,478 --> 00:02:33,239
That means your cat can hear the

74
00:02:33,089 --> 00:02:36,406
high-pitched squeaks of mice and other small

75
00:02:36,270 --> 00:02:39,086
animals that are completely silent to you.

76
00:02:40,044 --> 00:02:43,666
Each ear can rotate independently, almost one

77
00:02:43,530 --> 00:02:47,277
hundred eighty degrees, like two satellite dishes

78
00:02:47,141 --> 00:02:49,541
pointing at different sounds at the same

79
00:02:49,391 --> 00:02:49,972
time.

80
00:02:50,911 --> 00:02:52,589
Cats can also figure out where a

81
00:02:52,439 --> 00:02:55,908
sound is coming from with amazing precision.

82
00:02:56,217 --> 00:02:58,617
Useful when your dinner is hiding under

83
00:02:58,467 --> 00:02:59,825
a pile of leaves.

84
00:03:00,811 --> 00:03:02,044
And here's a fun one:

85
00:03:02,561 --> 00:03:05,086
a study in Japan found that cats

86
00:03:05,047 --> 00:03:06,919
can tell their owner's voice apart from

87
00:03:06,769 --> 00:03:07,711
a stranger's.

88
00:03:08,019 --> 00:03:09,433
They just don't always come when you

89
00:03:09,283 --> 00:03:09,864
call.

90
00:03:10,844 --> 00:03:14,300
Because their hearing is so sensitive, loud

91
00:03:14,164 --> 00:03:17,939
sounds like vacuum cleaners, fireworks or shouting

92
00:03:17,789 --> 00:03:19,744
can be really stressful for cats.

93
00:03:20,711 --> 00:03:21,792
Chapter three:

94
00:03:22,142 --> 00:03:22,889
smell.

95
00:03:23,267 --> 00:03:25,153
For a cat, the nose is like

96
00:03:25,003 --> 00:03:26,097
a newspaper.

97
00:03:26,392 --> 00:03:28,083
It's how it finds out who passed

98
00:03:27,933 --> 00:03:30,389
by and what happened while it was

99
00:03:30,253 --> 00:03:31,056
asleep.

100
00:03:32,011 --> 00:03:34,828
Cats have far more scent-detecting cells than

101
00:03:34,692 --> 00:03:35,300
we do.

102
00:03:35,581 --> 00:03:37,564
Some estimates go up to two hundred

103
00:03:37,414 --> 00:03:40,578
million, compared with about five million in

104
00:03:40,442 --> 00:03:41,328
humans.

105
00:03:42,278 --> 00:03:44,595
And they have a second smelling system:

106
00:03:44,986 --> 00:03:46,775
a special organ on the roof of

107
00:03:46,625 --> 00:03:49,511
the mouth, called the vomeronasal organ.

108
00:03:50,478 --> 00:03:52,975
When your cat sniffs something interesting and

109
00:03:52,825 --> 00:03:55,767
then freezes with its mouth slightly open,

110
00:03:56,048 --> 00:03:57,309
it's not disgusted.

111
00:03:57,589 --> 00:03:59,906
It's pushing the scent into that organ

112
00:03:59,756 --> 00:04:00,864
to analyze it.

113
00:04:01,858 --> 00:04:04,119
That funny face even has a name:

114
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:05,800
the flehmen response.

115
00:04:06,122 --> 00:04:08,661
Big cats like lions and tigers do

116
00:04:08,511 --> 00:04:09,175
it too.

117
00:04:10,111 --> 00:04:13,386
Smell is also how cats leave messages.

118
00:04:13,667 --> 00:04:15,914
They have scent glands on their cheeks,

119
00:04:15,986 --> 00:04:18,261
their paws and around the tail.

120
00:04:19,197 --> 00:04:21,681
When your cat scratches the sofa, it's

121
00:04:21,531 --> 00:04:23,708
not just sharpening its claws.

122
00:04:24,017 --> 00:04:25,806
It's also leaving a scent mark that

123
00:04:25,669 --> 00:04:26,486
says:

124
00:04:26,850 --> 00:04:27,931
I live here.

125
00:04:28,911 --> 00:04:32,422
Strong smells we find pleasant, like perfume,

126
00:04:32,772 --> 00:04:36,492
air fresheners or citrus, can be overwhelming

127
00:04:36,356 --> 00:04:38,033
for that powerful nose.

128
00:04:38,992 --> 00:04:39,948
Chapter four:

129
00:04:40,117 --> 00:04:41,114
the whiskers.

130
00:04:41,423 --> 00:04:43,517
They're not just cute decorations.

131
00:04:43,811 --> 00:04:47,809
Whiskers are super sensitive touch sensors, packed

132
00:04:47,659 --> 00:04:48,989
with nerves at the root.

133
00:04:49,958 --> 00:04:51,330
They help your cat judge if it

134
00:04:51,180 --> 00:04:53,289
can fit through a gap, because the

135
00:04:53,139 --> 00:04:55,261
whiskers on its face are roughly as

136
00:04:55,125 --> 00:04:56,594
wide as its body.

137
00:04:57,544 --> 00:05:00,347
Whiskers also feel tiny changes in the

138
00:05:00,197 --> 00:05:02,347
air, which helps a cat move in

139
00:05:02,197 --> 00:05:04,222
the dark and notice things around it

140
00:05:04,086 --> 00:05:05,180
without seeing them.

141
00:05:06,144 --> 00:05:08,516
Cats even have whiskers on the back

142
00:05:08,366 --> 00:05:09,572
of their front legs.

143
00:05:09,866 --> 00:05:12,975
They help them feel prey they're holding,

144
00:05:13,269 --> 00:05:15,516
right under their nose, where their eyes

145
00:05:15,366 --> 00:05:16,739
can't focus well.

146
00:05:17,678 --> 00:05:20,550
Some vets think deep, narrow bowls that

147
00:05:20,414 --> 00:05:22,592
brush against the whiskers can bother some

148
00:05:22,442 --> 00:05:23,231
cats.

149
00:05:23,539 --> 00:05:26,092
A wide, shallow bowl is an easy

150
00:05:25,942 --> 00:05:26,911
thing to try.

151
00:05:27,878 --> 00:05:29,070
And one important rule:

152
00:05:29,489 --> 00:05:31,361
never trim your cat's whiskers.

153
00:05:31,670 --> 00:05:33,250
It's like taking away part of its

154
00:05:33,100 --> 00:05:34,403
map of the world.

155
00:05:35,378 --> 00:05:36,459
Chapter five:

156
00:05:36,517 --> 00:05:37,223
taste.

157
00:05:37,545 --> 00:05:38,945
As we saw in another video on

158
00:05:38,795 --> 00:05:41,473
this channel, your cat can't taste sugar

159
00:05:41,323 --> 00:05:41,875
at all.

160
00:05:42,830 --> 00:05:45,147
Cats have only about four hundred and

161
00:05:44,997 --> 00:05:48,591
seventy taste buds, compared with around nine

162
00:05:48,441 --> 00:05:49,897
thousand in humans.

163
00:05:50,830 --> 00:05:53,508
Instead, their taste is tuned to meat.

164
00:05:53,816 --> 00:05:57,119
They're very sensitive to savory flavors, and

165
00:05:56,969 --> 00:05:59,702
they can also detect bitter tastes, which

166
00:05:59,552 --> 00:06:01,244
helps them avoid bad food.

167
00:06:02,197 --> 00:06:04,944
Many cats also seem to prefer fresh,

168
00:06:05,086 --> 00:06:06,069
moving water.

169
00:06:06,378 --> 00:06:08,833
That's why so many cats love drinking

170
00:06:08,683 --> 00:06:10,583
from the tap, or from a water

171
00:06:10,433 --> 00:06:11,028
fountain.

172
00:06:11,978 --> 00:06:13,239
Chapter six:

173
00:06:13,311 --> 00:06:14,517
touch and paws.

174
00:06:14,811 --> 00:06:17,059
Your cat's paw pads are full of

175
00:06:16,909 --> 00:06:18,059
nerve endings.

176
00:06:18,367 --> 00:06:21,989
They can feel textures, temperature and even

177
00:06:21,839 --> 00:06:23,503
vibrations through the ground.

178
00:06:24,444 --> 00:06:26,802
Cats also sweat through their paws.

179
00:06:27,097 --> 00:06:29,622
That's why you can sometimes see wet

180
00:06:29,486 --> 00:06:31,080
paw prints on the table at the

181
00:06:30,944 --> 00:06:31,497
vet.

182
00:06:31,805 --> 00:06:33,622
Your cat is nervous.

183
00:06:34,611 --> 00:06:37,997
That sensitivity is one reason many cats

184
00:06:37,972 --> 00:06:39,969
don't like having their paws touched.

185
00:06:40,306 --> 00:06:42,969
For them, it's a very personal spot.

186
00:06:43,944 --> 00:06:45,677
Their skin is sensitive too.

187
00:06:45,958 --> 00:06:48,525
Gentle, short strokes on the head and

188
00:06:48,389 --> 00:06:52,205
cheeks usually feel great, while long rubs

189
00:06:52,055 --> 00:06:54,414
along the back can get overwhelming.

190
00:06:55,364 --> 00:06:57,389
And when your cat taps something with

191
00:06:57,239 --> 00:06:59,431
its paw before touching it with its

192
00:06:59,281 --> 00:07:02,348
face, it's gathering information first.

193
00:07:02,642 --> 00:07:05,223
Paws are its hands and its sensors.

194
00:07:06,211 --> 00:07:07,208
Chapter seven:

195
00:07:07,336 --> 00:07:08,208
balance.

196
00:07:08,503 --> 00:07:11,278
Inside each ear, cats have a balance

197
00:07:11,142 --> 00:07:14,042
system that works like a super-precise level.

198
00:07:14,983 --> 00:07:17,800
Combined with a flexible spine, it lets

199
00:07:17,650 --> 00:07:20,453
them walk on narrow fences and land

200
00:07:20,303 --> 00:07:22,106
on their feet after a fall.

201
00:07:23,044 --> 00:07:24,139
The tail helps too.

202
00:07:24,433 --> 00:07:26,361
It works like the pole a tightrope

203
00:07:26,225 --> 00:07:29,250
walker uses, shifting weight to keep the

204
00:07:29,114 --> 00:07:30,125
body steady.

205
00:07:31,092 --> 00:07:34,381
That's why cats love high, narrow places

206
00:07:34,231 --> 00:07:36,798
like shelves and the backs of sofas.

207
00:07:37,092 --> 00:07:39,339
What looks risky to us is easy

208
00:07:39,189 --> 00:07:39,839
for them.

209
00:07:40,825 --> 00:07:43,406
But even with great balance, falls from

210
00:07:43,269 --> 00:07:45,600
windows and balconies are dangerous.

211
00:07:45,867 --> 00:07:48,586
Good balance is not the same as

212
00:07:48,436 --> 00:07:49,628
being able to fly.

213
00:07:50,578 --> 00:07:51,617
Chapter eight:

214
00:07:51,939 --> 00:07:53,214
the internal clock.

215
00:07:53,509 --> 00:07:56,478
Cats are amazing at learning routines.

216
00:07:56,759 --> 00:07:59,020
Many know exactly when it's dinner time,

217
00:07:59,300 --> 00:08:00,631
sometimes to the minute.

218
00:08:01,578 --> 00:08:03,353
They also notice when something in the

219
00:08:03,203 --> 00:08:05,464
routine changes, like a new piece of

220
00:08:05,314 --> 00:08:08,561
furniture, a new smell, or you coming

221
00:08:08,411 --> 00:08:09,256
home late.

222
00:08:10,225 --> 00:08:12,583
That's why changes like moving house or

223
00:08:12,433 --> 00:08:14,319
a new pet can stress a cat

224
00:08:14,169 --> 00:08:14,611
out.

225
00:08:14,906 --> 00:08:18,347
A predictable routine makes cats feel safe.

226
00:08:19,311 --> 00:08:20,517
So when your cat wakes you up

227
00:08:20,367 --> 00:08:23,058
at the same time every morning, it's

228
00:08:22,908 --> 00:08:24,322
not being dramatic.

229
00:08:24,617 --> 00:08:26,350
It just knows the schedule better than

230
00:08:26,214 --> 00:08:26,919
you do.

231
00:08:27,878 --> 00:08:28,709
Chapter nine:

232
00:08:29,198 --> 00:08:30,431
how to use all of this at

233
00:08:30,295 --> 00:08:30,820
home.

234
00:08:31,114 --> 00:08:33,820
Let's turn these senses into simple tips.

235
00:08:34,820 --> 00:08:35,859
For the eyes:

236
00:08:36,181 --> 00:08:38,498
choose toys that move in short, quick

237
00:08:38,361 --> 00:08:41,053
bursts, like a mouse running for cover.

238
00:08:41,361 --> 00:08:43,706
Movement is what turns on the hunter.

239
00:08:44,678 --> 00:08:46,620
And don't worry too much about color.

240
00:08:46,914 --> 00:08:49,828
A blue or green toy might actually

241
00:08:49,678 --> 00:08:51,842
stand out more to your cat than

242
00:08:51,692 --> 00:08:52,425
a red one.

243
00:08:53,420 --> 00:08:54,473
For the ears:

244
00:08:54,628 --> 00:08:56,167
give your cat a quiet place to

245
00:08:56,031 --> 00:08:59,292
hide during loud events, and keep music

246
00:08:59,142 --> 00:09:01,125
and TV at a comfortable volume.

247
00:09:02,134 --> 00:09:03,006
For the nose:

248
00:09:03,328 --> 00:09:05,089
keep the litter box away from the

249
00:09:04,939 --> 00:09:08,673
food bowl, avoid strong scented products, and

250
00:09:08,536 --> 00:09:10,756
let your cat sniff new things before

251
00:09:10,620 --> 00:09:12,131
they enter its space.

252
00:09:13,092 --> 00:09:14,075
For the whiskers:

253
00:09:14,189 --> 00:09:17,617
try wide, shallow food bowls, and never

254
00:09:17,481 --> 00:09:18,173
trim them.

255
00:09:19,144 --> 00:09:20,877
For the paws and balance:

256
00:09:21,158 --> 00:09:23,808
give your cat scratching posts and high

257
00:09:23,672 --> 00:09:26,044
places to climb, so it can mark

258
00:09:25,894 --> 00:09:28,391
its territory and watch the world from

259
00:09:28,241 --> 00:09:28,905
above.

260
00:09:29,878 --> 00:09:31,473
And for the internal clock:

261
00:09:31,823 --> 00:09:33,556
feed and play at about the same

262
00:09:33,406 --> 00:09:34,806
times every day.

263
00:09:35,086 --> 00:09:36,820
Routine is comfort.

264
00:09:37,783 --> 00:09:39,433
So the next time your cat stares

265
00:09:39,283 --> 00:09:41,892
at an empty corner, freezes with its

266
00:09:41,756 --> 00:09:44,128
mouth open, or ignores you when you

267
00:09:43,978 --> 00:09:45,544
call, remember:

268
00:09:46,006 --> 00:09:47,725
it's living in a world full of

269
00:09:47,589 --> 00:09:51,642
sounds, smells and movements you can't sense.

270
00:09:52,611 --> 00:09:53,733
Your cat isn't weird.

271
00:09:54,028 --> 00:09:55,969
It's just running on a completely different

272
00:09:55,819 --> 00:09:57,039
set of senses.

273
00:09:58,025 --> 00:10:00,439
Which of these cat superpowers surprised you

274
00:10:00,289 --> 00:10:01,050
the most?

275
00:10:01,344 --> 00:10:03,411
Tell me in the comments, and I'll

276
00:10:03,275 --> 00:10:04,981
see you in the next video.
